\frametitle{The prompts are not composed from scratch (2/2)}
\textbf{$\Sigma_C$ --- in between, and the direct precedent for
  $\rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$.} \citet{mao2024fitrag} describe their instruction as
  consisting of \emph{three parts}, with a stated rationale per part: direct
  the model to the passages; ask it to read the question carefully; ask for an
  answer \emph{plus} a justification.
\begin{lstlisting}[style=promptbox]
Refer to the passage below and answer the following question.
Make sure you fully understand the meaning of the question and passages.
Then give the answer and explain why you choose this answer.
Passages: {passages}
Question: {question}
\end{lstlisting}
  \medskip
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Writing a prompt as an \emph{enumerated set of requirements with a
          purpose per requirement} is exactly what $\rho(\Sigma,\varphi)$
          formalises. \citet{mao2024fitrag} report this form outperforms both
          a bare instruction and a chain-of-thought variant on the same
          passages.
    \item \textbf{No requirement checked here is an invention of this thesis:}
          the citation constraint is the central requirement of
          \citet{gao2023alce}; the justification constraint is the third part
          of \citet{mao2024fitrag}. That is why adherence can be checked by
          \emph{parsing the output} rather than by judgement.
  \end{itemize}
